# Line endings.
#
# This is a Linux application and every text file in the repository is stored
# with LF. Without this file the form a checkout takes is decided entirely by
# each clone's core.autocrlf, which on Windows means CRLF in the working tree.
#
# That is what breaks `bundle exec rake` in the Docker image: PATH resolves
# /opt/postal/app/bin ahead of the system gems, so the binstub is used, and a
# CRLF shebang dies with "/usr/bin/env: 'ruby\r': No such file or directory"
# before it runs anything. Dockerfile.production and
# script/production/normalize-crlf.sh both exist only to undo that after the
# fact. Marking the affected files here removes the cause instead.
#
# `text=auto` preserves the existing behaviour for everything else -- LF in the
# repository, and whatever core.autocrlf asks for in the working tree -- so
# adding this file does not silently rewrite a checkout that already exists.
# Only the patterns below change the checkout form, and only on a fresh checkout
# or an explicit re-normalization.
* text=auto

# The bin binstubs are the files that actually fail. They have no extension, so
# they need naming explicitly.
bin/* text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are read by sh and bash, neither of which tolerates a carriage
# return -- least of all in the shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The deploy tooling and the image entrypoint are executed, not sourced.
deploy/**/*.sh text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Never normalize anything binary. `text=auto` should detect these correctly,
# but an explicit marker is cheaper than a corrupted image.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.key binary
*.pdf binary
